Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and how they could be used  

• "abstract_localized": Variant column that stores the abstract text(s) for each publication. Needed to look for the phrase “internet of things”.  
• "country_code": Text column. Filter to U.S. publications → "country_code" = 'US'.  
• "filing_date": Number column in YYYYMMDD format. Used to derive the filing month (e.g. INT("filing_date"/100) gives YYYYMM).  
• "publication_number": Text column. Allows us to list / count distinct publications.  
• "application_number_formatted": Useful if you also want the formatted U.S. application number.  
• "family_id": Lets you de‑duplicate by simple family if necessary.  

Typical conditions  

1. LATERAL FLATTEN on "abstract_localized" to reach each language‑specific JSON object.  
2. Filter on a.value:"text"::STRING ILIKE '%internet%of%things%'.  
3. Filter "country_code" = 'US'.  
4. Restrict "filing_date" between 20080101 and 20221231 (2008‑2022).  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (LIMIT 20 each) that progress from simple inspection to building the required monthly count.  
All identifiers are enclosed in double quotation marks and each query is preceded by an explanatory comment.

```sql
-- 1. Quick look at the PUBLICATIONS table structure (first 20 rows, no filters).
SELECT 
    "publication_number",
    "country_code",
    "filing_date",
    "abstract_localized"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. See just the U.S. publications filed in the target period (2008‑2022).
SELECT 
    "publication_number",
    "filing_date"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "country_code" = 'US'
  AND "filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Flatten the abstract JSON and show any record whose abstract contains
--    the phrase “internet of things” (case‑insensitive fuzzy match).
SELECT 
    p."publication_number",
    p."filing_date",
    f.value:"language"::STRING AS "lang",
    f.value:"text"::STRING   AS "abstract_text"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."abstract_localized") f
WHERE p."country_code" = 'US'
  AND p."filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
  AND f.value:"text"::STRING ILIKE '%internet%of%things%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. List distinct publication numbers that match the IoT phrase.
SELECT DISTINCT 
    p."publication_number"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."abstract_localized") f
WHERE p."country_code" = 'US'
  AND p."filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
  AND f.value:"text"::STRING ILIKE '%internet%of%things%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Check which languages the matching abstracts are written in.
SELECT DISTINCT 
    f.value:"language"::STRING AS "lang"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."abstract_localized") f
WHERE p."country_code" = 'US'
  AND p."filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
  AND f.value:"text"::STRING ILIKE '%internet%of%things%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Retrieve filing year and month (YYYYMM) for each matching publication.
SELECT 
    p."publication_number",
    FLOOR(p."filing_date" / 100)::INTEGER AS "filing_yyyymm"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."abstract_localized") f
WHERE p."country_code" = 'US'
  AND p."filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
  AND f.value:"text"::STRING ILIKE '%internet%of%things%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Get the first 20 family IDs associated with the IoT‑matching publications.
SELECT DISTINCT 
    p."family_id"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."abstract_localized") f
WHERE p."country_code" = 'US'
  AND p."filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
  AND f.value:"text"::STRING ILIKE '%internet%of%things%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Count IoT‑matching U.S. publications per filing month (YYYYMM).
SELECT 
    FLOOR(p."filing_date" / 100)::INTEGER        AS "filing_yyyymm",
    COUNT(DISTINCT p."publication_number")       AS "pub_count"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."abstract_localized") f
WHERE p."country_code" = 'US'
  AND p."filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
  AND f.value:"text"::STRING ILIKE '%internet%of%things%'
GROUP BY "filing_yyyymm"
ORDER BY "filing_yyyymm"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Same as #8 but keep all months (200801–202212) even if zero publications.
--    Uses a derived calendar table created on the fly via GENERATOR.
WITH cal AS (
    SELECT
        (200801 + seq4()) AS "yyyymm"
    FROM TABLE(GENERATOR(ROWCOUNT => (202212 - 200801 + 1)))
),
iot AS (
    SELECT 
        FLOOR(p."filing_date" / 100)::INTEGER AS "yyyymm",
        COUNT(DISTINCT p."publication_number") AS "cnt"
    FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."abstract_localized") f
    WHERE p."country_code" = 'US'
      AND p."filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
      AND f.value:"text"::STRING ILIKE '%internet%of%things%'
    GROUP BY "yyyymm"
)
SELECT 
    cal."yyyymm",
    COALESCE(iot."cnt", 0) AS "iot_pub_count"
FROM cal
LEFT JOIN iot
  ON cal."yyyymm" = iot."yyyymm"
ORDER BY cal."yyyymm"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Produce the full answer set (all months 2008‑01 to 2022‑12) in one go.
WITH cal AS (
    SELECT (200801 + seq4()) AS "yyyymm"
    FROM TABLE(GENERATOR(ROWCOUNT => (202212 - 200801 + 1)))
),
iot AS (
    SELECT 
        FLOOR(p."filing_date" / 100)::INTEGER AS "yyyymm",
        COUNT(DISTINCT p."publication_number") AS "cnt"
    FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."abstract_localized") f
    WHERE p."country_code" = 'US'
      AND p."filing_date" BETWEEN 20080101 AND 20221231
      AND f.value:"text"::STRING ILIKE '%internet%of%things%'
    GROUP BY "yyyymm"
)
SELECT 
    cal."yyyymm",
    COALESCE(iot."cnt", 0) AS "iot_pub_count"
FROM cal
LEFT JOIN iot
  ON cal."yyyymm" = iot."yyyymm"
ORDER BY cal."yyyymm";
```
